==================================================
Lab 18: SQL Injection with Filter Bypass via XML
Encoding
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab combines two ideas we’ve already used separately: the single-column concatenation trick from lab 6,
and a brand new problem, a filter (commonly called a WAF, short for Web Application Firewall) that actively
blocks requests containing obvious SQL injection patterns. The twist here is that our input isn’t sent as a
normal URL parameter or cookie, it’s sent inside an XML document. XML has its own way of representing characters
called entities, and we can use this to sneak our payload past the filter without the filter ever recognizing it
as SQL.

Goal: find a way to write a UNION-based SQL injection payload that a filter won’t recognize and block, then use
it to steal usernames and passwords.

==============================
WHERE THE INJECTION POINT IS

This lab has a “Check stock” feature on each product page. When clicked, it sends a request containing two
values, a productId and a storeId, formatted as XML rather than as typical URL parameters. The storeId value is
the one that ends up inside a SQL query without being checked or cleaned, which we can confirm by replacing it
with a simple math expression. If the application responds based on the result of that expression instead of
treating it as plain text, this proves the value is actually being evaluated by the database.

=============================
STEP 1: CONFIRM THE INJECTION POINT WORKS

Before trying anything related to SQL injection specifically, we first confirm that storeId is being evaluated
rather than just compared as plain text. Submitting something like one plus one instead of a real store ID, and
seeing the application respond as though we had submitted the number two, confirms the value is being calculated
and used directly, not just matched exactly against stored text.

=============================
STEP 2: HIT THE FILTER

Knowing the column-counting trick from earlier labs, we try submitting a UNION SELECT with a single NULL value.
Normally, this is a harmless, standard first step. However, this time the request gets blocked entirely, with
the application flagging it as a potential attack. This tells us a filter is actively watching for suspicious
patterns like the word UNION or SELECT, and rejecting any request containing them outright, regardless of
whether the request would have actually caused any harm.

=============================
STEP 3: UNDERSTAND XML ENTITIES

Since our data is being sent as XML, we can take advantage of a feature built into the XML format itself. XML
allows any character to be written not just as its normal plain form, but also using something called a
character entity, which represents that exact same character using a numeric code instead. A hex entity looks
like an ampersand, followed by a hash symbol, an x, a hexadecimal number representing the character, and a
semicolon at the end. For example, the letter U can be written using its numeric code instead of the plain
letter.

Crucially, the XML parser that reads and processes our request will automatically convert these entities back
into their real characters before passing the value along further into the application. So if we encode our
entire payload this way, the actual text that leaves our side looks nothing like SQL at all, it looks like a
jumble of entity codes, but by the time it reaches the database, it has already been decoded back into the real
SQL text we intended.

=============================
STEP 4: BYPASS THE FILTER

Because the filter is likely scanning the raw request for literal suspicious text such as the word UNION or a
stray quote character, encoding our entire payload into hex entities means none of those literal patterns ever
appear in what the filter actually sees. The filter only inspects the request as it is sent, before the XML
parser has had a chance to decode anything, so it has no way of recognizing our intent. Once decoded internally
by the application, the real SQL injection payload reappears fully intact and reaches the database as normal.

Resending the same column-counting attempt, but now with every character hex-encoded, goes through without being
blocked, confirming the filter has been successfully bypassed.

=============================
STEP 5: FIND THE REAL COLUMN COUNT

Continuing the investigation, we discover that this particular query only returns a single column. Attempting
to return more than one column causes the application to show a zero units response instead, indicating an
error behind the scenes. This is different from some earlier labs where multiple columns were available, so
here, absolutely everything we want to extract has to fit into just that one single column.

=============================
STEP 6: COMBINE USERNAME AND PASSWORD INTO ONE COLUMN

Since there is only one usable column, we rely on the exact same string concatenation idea used in lab six.
Username and password are joined together using the double pipe symbol, with a tilde character placed in
between them as a separator, so the combined result can later be split back into its two original parts once we
read it from the response.

This entire concatenated SQL expression, same as before, gets converted into hex entities before being placed
inside the storeId field in the XML body, ensuring the filter never sees anything it would recognize as an
attack.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and use Burp Suite to intercept the stock check request.
Confirm the storeId value is evaluated, by submitting a simple math expression instead of a real store ID.
Attempt a basic UNION SELECT NULL payload, and observe that it gets blocked by the filter.
Convert the same payload into hex entities, and resend it, confirming that it is no longer blocked.
Discover that only one column is returned, since attempting more results in a zero units response.
Build a payload selecting username concatenated with password, separated by a tilde, from the users table,
hex-encode this entire payload, and place it inside the storeId field.
Send the request, and read the leaked username and password pair, separated by the tilde, directly from the
response.
Log in manually as administrator using the leaked password to solve the lab.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, used for sending web requests.

It asks for the base address of the lab, and builds the full stock-check endpoint address by appending the
specific path this lab uses for that feature.

A helper function called to_hex_entities is defined. It goes through every single character in a given piece of
text, one at a time, and converts each one into its hex entity form, joining all these converted pieces back
together into one long string. This is what actually performs the XML-based obfuscation trick, turning an
entire SQL payload into a form the filter cannot recognize.

Another helper function called build_xml is defined, which takes a product ID and whatever storeId payload we
want to test, and returns a complete, properly formatted XML document string, matching the exact structure this
lab’s stock-check feature expects.

The script then performs four separate steps, one after another, each sending a request and printing the result,
so progress can be checked at every stage rather than only seeing the final outcome.

The first step sends one plus one as the storeId value, to confirm the application evaluates it as a
calculation rather than treating it as plain text, matching the very first manual confirmation step.

The second step sends a plain, unencoded UNION SELECT NULL payload, expecting this one to be blocked by the
filter, purely to visually confirm the filter is active before trying to bypass it.

The third step takes that exact same UNION SELECT NULL payload, but passes it through the hex entity conversion
function first, then sends it. This should now succeed without being blocked, confirming the filter bypass
works.

The fourth and final step builds the real data-stealing payload, selecting username concatenated with password,
separated by a tilde, from the users table, converts this entire string into hex entities, and sends it as the
storeId value. The response is then checked for the presence of the tilde character, since that symbol only
appears in our own injected, combined value, not in any normal stock response. If found, the leaked line
containing the username and password is printed directly.

=============================
WHY THE FILTER CANNOT STOP THIS

A WAF or input filter in situations like this usually works by scanning the raw request content for specific
known dangerous patterns, such as the literal word UNION, the word SELECT, or an unescaped quote character. By
encoding our entire payload using XML hex entities, none of these literal patterns exist anywhere in the request
the filter actually inspects. The dangerous content only reappears after the XML parser decodes the entities,
which happens later in the application’s own processing, completely outside of what the filter is able to see or
control.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying SQL injection flaw is the same as every previous lab, unsanitized input reaching a SQL query.
What makes this lab different is the presence of a filter meant to catch and block exactly this kind of attack,
combined with the fact that XML, as a data format, has a built-in, standard way of representing any character
indirectly. This combination creates a gap between what the filter can see and what the application actually
ends up processing.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that no amount of clever encoding or obfuscation can ever change the structure of
the SQL query, since the database itself never interprets user input as code regardless of its form.

Other good practices:

Avoid relying only on filters that scan for specific known bad patterns, since encoding tricks like this one are
a well known and common way to slip past this kind of surface-level defense.
Any filtering or validation should be applied only after the input has been fully decoded into its final,
real form, not before, so that entities, encodings, or other obfuscation techniques cannot be used to hide
malicious content from the filter.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

A filter that only scans raw, undecoded input can be bypassed using any encoding scheme the underlying format
supports, since the real content only becomes visible after decoding happens.
XML entities are a legitimate, standard part of the XML format, not a hack or exploit by themselves, which is
exactly why a filter cannot simply ban their use outright without breaking normal XML functionality.
Filters and blocklists that look for specific bad patterns are often weaker than they appear, since they only
catch attacks in the exact form the filter’s author anticipated.
The safest defense remains preventing the SQL injection at its root, through parameterized queries, rather than
trying to catch and block malicious-looking input after the fact.
